WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.680
A seven year old arrives home with her first
report card from school, which is full of

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.200
terrible grades.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.840
Her dad leans over her shoulder to take a
peek at the academic horror show.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.160
The girl turns around and asks: So what do
you think dad?

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.680
Nature or nurture?

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.880
Centuries ago, John Locke and others, began
arguing that our personality is mainly shaped

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.960
by the environment we were raised in.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.400
Others, such as Charles Darwin, believed that
we are largely programmed genetically.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.400
Modern behavioral geneticists think that it’s
more complex - because the two interact.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.960
And relatively recently, a little crayfish
from Germany, showed just how little we actually

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.440
know — but more about her later.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.640
First, let's go back to the girl, her dad
and what we do know.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:07.120
Neither the dad, nor the smartest scientists,
can answer what leads to certain school outcomes

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.560
of our 7-year old girl.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.800
We do know, however, a bit about the influences
of genes and the environment when it comes

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.200
to groups of specific populations.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.680
Take, for example, a group of 100 white American
men in their early twenties and you will probably

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:35.280
find, on average, an IQ of 100, a height of
178 cm or around 5.8 feet, and that ten have

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.320
spelling problems.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.640
We can now ask which of these 3 traits is
determined by genes — anything that happened

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:49.200
before conception, often referred to as nature;
and which by the environment— anything that

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.840
happened after their mothers got pregnant,
often referred to as nurture.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.360
To understand the two forces,&nbsp;
we need to acknowledge&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.320
that environmental influences are often random

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.680
— siblings experience home very differently.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.240
And we need to recognize that genes are complex
— there is no single gene doing a single

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:12.480
thing.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:18.480
We therefore know little about which trait
in an individual is the result of their genetic

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.760
makeup and which is due to the environment.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:27.680
We only know about the impact of nature and
nurture on trait differences of a particular

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.400
group.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.480
And to learn about that, we need to understand
heritability.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.360
Heritability is a factor that ranges between
one and zero.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.760
One stands for genetic influences.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.480
Zero for environmental factors.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.360
Heritability defines the genetic impact on
trait differences in a particular population.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.720
It can not measure the degree to which a trait
of a particular individual is genetic.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.520
Dyslexia, for example, has a heritability
closer to 1.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.800
That means that genes explain more of the
difference in spelling mistakes of our group

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.840
than school, family or random factors.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.080
It does not mean that one particular person
with spelling problems got them genetically.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.040
Some just didn’t get much support when they
were young.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.360
How complex the interaction between genes
and environment really is becomes clear when

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.680
we look at something as seemingly straightforward
as IQ and height.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.240
First, let’s examine height.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.920
A group of 100 white American male students
are on average 178 centimeters tall — that’s

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.920
roughly five feet, 10 inches.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.400
Their heritability for height is around 0.8.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:04:00.000
If some men in this group are 183 cm — or
six feet tall, then heritability allows us

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.640
to assume that 80% of that difference is,
on average, caused by genetic variants and

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.520
20% of the difference is due to lifestyle.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:17.040
If the same group of people were raised in
a region that experienced systemic droughts

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:23.600
and they never had enough to eat, their potential
for an average height of 178 centimeters is

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.440
far from ever being reached.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.920
The heritability for height might now go from
0.8 down to 0.5.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.200
Nature and nurture are now equally responsible
for group differences.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.400
This means, heritability changes.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.160
If we have a well nourished population on
the one side and a malnourished group on the

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.920
other, then the impact of nutrition on the
differences in height changes from 20% to

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:54.320
50%.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:01.200
In other words, nurture matters a lot, until
we reach a point from which it's not as relevant.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.360
So what about intelligence?

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:13.120
The heritability of IQ is around 0.6 for people
in their twenties and then increases as we

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:13.440
age.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.080
The average IQ in our group will be around
100.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.800
But some have an IQ of 110.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:27.200
Genes are therefore responsible for 60% of
the difference, and random factors and the

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.120
environment for about 40%.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:37.680
In other words, for those with an IQ of 110,
6 of the additional 10 points, can, on average,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.360
be attributed to nature and 4 points to nurture.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.640
Now even if nurture plays a big role, it doesn't
mean that teachers or parents had any impact.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.880
Unlike height, which we know how to increase
through nutrition, when it comes to IQ we

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.040
don’t know much about how to improve it.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.480
Random factors seem to play a big role.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.720
How random the environment is, becomes clear
when we look at two kids that are born into

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:11.760
the same family.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.000
Both are raised in the same shared environment,
but firstborns, on average, turn out to be

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.720
more intelligent than their younger siblings.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.480
And if one of two siblings was adopted, the
two would have some correlation in IQ during

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.600
their upbringing, but as adults they would
be hardly more similar in intelligence than

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.880
complete strangers.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.160
Parents, it seems, have a very limited impact
on a child's IQ.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.880
So now what about that crayfish?

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.000
In 1995 German scientists made an odd observation.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.600
The little female creature had mutated and
become asexual — able to make clones of

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:54.080
itself.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:07:00.960
As each clone shared exactly the same genetic
blueprint, the scientists set up an experiment

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:02.400
for the ultimate test.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:09.680
Hundreds of these little identical creatures
were placed in the same environment.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.200
Same water, same temperature, same amount
of food.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:19.520
Even though the scientists tried to control
all these variables, the unthinkable happened.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.280
Some crayfish stayed tiny, others grew big.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.040
Some died, others lived on and on.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:31.120
And while some became social, others enjoyed
solitude.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.440
Why do you think that happened?

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:39.920
Is that nature, nurture or a complex interplay
of the two with some random variations in

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:41.760
replicated genetic code?

